{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, extraneous candidate x=-9; the true root is x=26","reason":"Squaring x+4=5√(x+10) gives (x-26)(x+9)=0. At x=-9 the original left side is 1-6=-5, whereas x=26 gives 6-1=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"positive-radical-substitution","label":"Substitute a positive radical variable","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-radical-substitution":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_040","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:isolate-and-square:dcb474b47e30","label":"Clear the denominator and square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isolate-and-square":["math:radical-equations","cnm_168","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_168","cnm_224","cnm_167","cnm_040","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: no root","reason":"The real domain is x≥3. There √(x+4)>√(x-3), so the left side is greater than 1 and cannot equal zero.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q42","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_148","cnm_042"],"id":"solution:compare-the-radicals:6143e6632102","label":"Compare the radicals and check possible nonreal roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-the-radicals":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079","cnm_168"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079","cnm_168","cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_148","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, -64","reason":"Equal radicands give t² - 5t - 24 = (t - 8)(t + 3) = 0. Since t ≥ 0, t = 8, so x = ±8. Both satisfy the original equation, and their product is -64.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_008","cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"absolute-value-substitution","label":"Solve for the absolute value first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"absolute-value-substitution":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224","cnm_008","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, one real root, x=3","reason":"The original equation requires 2≤x≤4. Squaring √(x−2)=4−x gives (x−3)(x−6)=0; only x=3 satisfies the original equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1950_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_039"],"id":"compare-with-three","label":"Compare possible roots with 3","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-with-three":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079","cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 4x²-17x+4=0","reason":"For x>0, multiplying by √x gives 2x+2=5√x. Squaring yields 4x²-17x+4=0. Its roots 4 and 1/4 both satisfy the original equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h039","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"],"id":"solution:clear-and-square-the-radical:0a2c1cc59005","label":"Clear the denominator and square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"clear-and-square-the-radical":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148","cnm_036","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148","cnm_036","cnm_224","cnm_h039","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h039","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"],"id":"solution:substitute-the-square-root:0a2c1cc59005","label":"Use the square root as the unknown","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-the-square-root":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224","cnm_146","cnm_h039","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, x=1","reason":"The domain requires x≥1. Then √(5x-1)≥2 and √(x-1)≥0, with their sum equal to 2 only at x=1. Direct substitution verifies that value.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_039"],"id":"domain-and-bound","label":"Use the domain to bound the sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"domain-and-bound":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079","cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 4x=5","reason":"Rearranging gives √(x+1)=√(x-1)+1. Squaring yields 2√(x-1)=1, so x=5/4. The original left side is 1/2-3/2+1=0, and therefore 4x=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"isolate-and-square","label":"Isolate a square root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isolate-and-square":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148","cnm_042","cnm_167","cnm_168"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_042"],"id":"sum-and-difference","label":"Find the sum and difference of the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-and-difference":["math:radical-equations","cnm_147","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_147","cnm_090","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 2 roots, x=1 and x=5","reason":"The domain is x<=5, and the equation is (1-x)*sqrt(5-x)=0. Its two solutions are x=1 and x=5, both valid in the original equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"zero-factor-case-split","label":"Separate the zero-radical case","methodDirectNodeIds":{"zero-factor-case-split":["math:radical-equations","cnm_074","cnm_040"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_074","cnm_040"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-factor-check","label":"Square, factor, and check","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-factor-check":["math:radical-equations","cnm_168","cnm_151","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_168","cnm_151","cnm_152"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_039"],"id":"zero-radical-case","label":"Separate the zero square root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"zero-radical-case":["math:radical-equations","cnm_040"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_040","cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, x=1/8","reason":"Set t=√(2x)≥0. Then 2t²+t-1=(2t-1)(t+1)=0, so t=1/2 and x=1/8. Indeed, 4(1/8)+√(1/4)=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_003"],"id":"compare-with-one-half","label":"Compare both terms with one half","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-with-one-half":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_079","cnm_096","cnm_167","cnm_074","cnm_003"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 49","reason":"Squaring gives 2+√x=9, so √x=7 and x=49. Substitution yields √(2+7)=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"remove-radical-layers","label":"Remove the square roots one at a time","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-radical-layers":["math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: ab = 0 and a+b >= 0","reason":"The square root forces a+b >= 0, and squaring gives a²+b² = a²+2ab+b², hence ab = 0. Conversely, these conditions give sqrt(a²+b²) = sqrt((a+b)²) = |a+b| = a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-and-check-signs","label":"Square and check the signs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-check-signs":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148","cnm_168"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, no root greater than 3","reason":"The respective real solution sets are {-3,3}, {0,2/3}, and {3}. In the third equation, squaring also gives -2, but it violates the radical domains. Every valid root is at most 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-root-branches","label":"Use equal and opposite branches","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-root-branches":["cnm_220","cnm_042","math:radical-equations","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_220","cnm_042","math:radical-equations","cnm_224"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-the-quadratics","label":"Use difference-of-squares factoring","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-the-quadratics":["cnm_152","cnm_224","math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_152","cnm_224","math:radical-equations"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_075","cnm_167","cnm_029","cnm_034"],"id":"real-solution-sets","label":"Find the real solution sets","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-solution-sets":["cnm_039","cnm_220","cnm_042","cnm_224","math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_039","cnm_220","cnm_042","cnm_224","math:radical-equations","cnm_075","cnm_167","cnm_029","cnm_034"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, c = b(a² - 1)/a","reason":"Squaring gives a + b/c = a²b/c. Multiplication by c yields ac = b(a² - 1), equivalent to C. Conversely, that condition makes the positive squared quantities equal.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q42","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"],"id":"square-and-rearrange","label":"Use positivity to square reversibly","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-rearrange":["math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, (√(4a-3)-1)/2","reason":"Set y=√(a+x). Then x≥0, x²+y=a, and y²-x=a. Subtracting yields (y-x)(y+x)=y+x. Since y+x>0, y=x+1, so x²+x+1=a. Its unique nonnegative root is (√(4a-3)-1)/2, and substitution verifies it.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_222"],"id":"introduce-inner-square-root","label":"Introduce the inner square root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"introduce-inner-square-root":["math:radical-equations","cnm_147"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_147","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_222"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_222"],"id":"square-and-filter-factors","label":"Square twice and use the original signs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-filter-factors":["math:radical-equations","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_146","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_222"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 11","reason":"Set √(120 - √x) = k ≥ 0. Then √x = 120 - k² ≥ 0, so k can be any integer from 0 through 10. Each gives exactly one distinct valid value x = (120 - k²)², yielding 11 values.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"count-integer-outputs","label":"Count possible integer outputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-integer-outputs":["math:radical-equations","cnm_h020"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_h020","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3 pairs","reason":"Since 1984 = 31·8², the integer solutions have x = 31u² and y = 31v² with positive integers u + v = 8. The condition u < v permits u = 1, 2, 3, giving exactly three pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_169","cnm_168"],"id":"identify-common-radical-factor","label":"Use integrality to identify the radical factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"identify-common-radical-factor":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:radical-equations","math:prime-factorization","cnm_169","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 51.005","reason":"Writing s=sqrt(x^2-1), (x+s)(x-s)=1 gives x+s=10 and x-s=1/10. Thus x=101/20. The requested expression equals 2x^2=10201/200=51.005.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"],"id":"use-reciprocal-pairs","label":"Use reciprocal pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-reciprocal-pairs":["math:radical-equations","cnm_173","cnm_147","cnm_016"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_173","cnm_147","cnm_016","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 11","reason":"If the expression equals n, then sqrt(x)=120-n^2, requiring n to be an integer with 0<=n<=10. Each such n gives one distinct valid x=(120-n^2)^2, so there are 11 values.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_10A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"count-integer-outputs","label":"Count the possible integer outputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-integer-outputs":["math:radical-equations","cnm_h020","math:bijective-counting"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_h020","math:bijective-counting","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 595","reason":"Squaring shows that √(xy) is rational, hence an integer. Since 1183 = 7·13², the common squarefree part must be 7: write x = 7u² and y = 7v² with positive integers u+v = 13. Their squared sum is minimized at {u,v} = {6,7}, giving x+y = 7(36+49) = 595.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-radical-quadratic","label":"Reduce the radicals and minimize","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-radical-quadratic":["math:radical-equations","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:prime-factorization","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:prime-factorization","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, x^2 = 80","reason":"Let u = ∛(x+9) and v = ∛(x-9). Then u-v = 3 and 18 = u^3-v^3 = 27+9uv, so uv = -1. Cubing gives x^2-81 = -1, hence x^2 = 80. The candidates x = ±4√5 satisfy the original equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_076","cnm_143","cnm_147","cnm_074","cnm_146"],"id":"cube-root-product","label":"Find the product of the cube roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-root-product":["math:radical-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_076","cnm_143","cnm_147","cnm_074","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 3 4/7; x=25/7","reason":"The west route is sqrt(15^2+8^2)+x+8=x+25. Thus the east-gate distance is x+16, giving (x+16)^2-(x+9)^2=225. Factoring yields 7(2x+25)=225, so x=25/7, which satisfies the original distance equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"radical-distance-equation","label":"Equate the two route lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"radical-distance-equation":["math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 8","reason":"Let a = sqrt(49 - x^2) and b = sqrt(25 - x^2). Then 3(a + b) = (a - b)(a + b) = 49 - 25 = 24, so a + b = 8. The resulting a = 11/2 and b = 5/2 are realizable with x^2 = 75/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"isolate-and-square","label":"Isolate one radical and square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isolate-and-square":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1<x<2; x=(1+√5)/2","reason":"The finite truncations increase and stay below 2, so they converge. Their positive limit satisfies x=√(1+x), hence x²-x-1=0 and x=(1+√5)/2, which lies between 1 and 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The bound-only route does not require the exact fixed point; solution routes are alternatives."],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q42","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-fixed-point","label":"Convergent truncations and exact positive fixed point","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-fixed-point":["math:radical-equations","cnm_222","cnm_079"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_222","cnm_079"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_147"],"id":"fixed-point-with-error-bound","label":"Find a stable value and bound the error","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fixed-point-with-error-bound":["math:recursive-infinite-expressions","math:radical-equations","cnm_222","cnm_079"]},"nodeIds":["math:recursive-infinite-expressions","math:radical-equations","cnm_222","cnm_079","cnm_h200","cnm_167","cnm_168","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. x=hd/(2h+d)","reason":"Pythagoras gives (h+d-x)^2=(h+x)^2+d^2, which reduces to hd=x(2h+d). Thus x=hd/(2h+d). For positive h and d, this gives x>0 and MA=h+d-x>0, so the squaring is valid.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"pythagorean-equation","label":"Pythagorean length equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pythagorean-equation":["cnm_174","math:radical-equations","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","math:radical-equations","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 14; a/b=7+4sqrt(3).","reason":"For r=a/b>1, r+1=4sqrt(r), giving r^2-14r+1=0. The admissible root is 7+4sqrt(3), approximately 13.928, which rounds to 14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1968_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-ratio-equation","label":"Square the ratio equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-ratio-equation":["math:radical-equations","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_222"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 6/23","reason":"Place the radius-1, radius-2, and radius-3 centers at (0,0), (3,0), and (0,4). For inner center (x,y), subtracting squared-distance equations gives x=1-r/3 and y=1-r/2. Substitution into x²+y²=(r+1)² yields (23r-6)(r+6)=0. The positive radius is 6/23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"heron-area-balance","label":"Split the center triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"heron-area-balance":["math:heron-formula","cnm_p150","math:radical-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:heron-formula","cnm_p150","math:radical-equations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 3; b=(1+√33)/2 ≈ 3.372","reason":"The common perimeter is 18 and area is 12. Therefore a=9-b/2 and b²(9-b)=64, giving (b-8)(b²-b-8)=0. Excluding the congruent case b=8 and the negative root leaves b=(1+√33)/2, closest to 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"altitude-area-equation","label":"Altitude and triangle area","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-area-equation":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","math:radical-equations","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","math:radical-equations","cnm_222"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_111","cnm_112","cnm_113"],"id":"altitude-area-known-root","label":"Use the altitude and remove the known solution","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-area-known-root":["cnm_137","cnm_174","math:radical-equations","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","math:radical-equations","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_222","cnm_p148","cnm_111","cnm_112","cnm_113"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:radical-equations","status":"mapped","total":27}
